# And This Is Called a Devotional Work!

After performing, together with Appar at Vedaranyam, the divine sport of making the temple doors there open and close, Sambandar accepts the invitation sent from Madurai by Kulachirai and Mangayarkkarasiyar. He sets out for Madurai to wage a religious contest against the Jains there, defeat them, and establish Saivism.

As soon as Sambandar leaves Vedaranyam, the Jains in Madurai have fiery nightmares, while more and more auspicious omens appear to Kulachirai and Mangayarkkarasiyar. Sekkizhar sings of the Jains said to have had those evil dreams, abusing them to his heart’s content with cruel expressions unfit even to be uttered:

“Base men with filthy bodies,”

“Deceitful outcastes,”

“Men without honour,”

“Sinners,”

“Evil-doers,”

“Jain thugs,”

“Senseless Jains,”

“Mean-headed Jains,”

“Jains of cruel deeds,”

and many more.

When I say that Sekkizhar abused the Jains, some Saivite “true” devotees may become angry with me. They may say, “Sekkizhar, clothed in compassion, the noble man who governed by the path of love, could never have degraded the Jains with such words. This is false.” But if such people would look through the *Periyapuranam* sung by Sekkizhar even once, they would find abuse far more degrading than the words cited here. They would understand that wherever the *Periyapuranam* speaks of Jains, Sekkizhar seems unable to sing without attaching such insults to them.

Does speaking so degradingly of another accord with Tamil tradition and culture? No. Why, then, did Sekkizhar turn the *Periyapuranam* into a dictionary of abuse?

> To speak unpleasant words when pleasant ones are at hand  
> Is like eating an unripe fruit when ripe fruit is available.

That is Tamil culture. Why did Sekkizhar, heir to Tamil tradition, fail to know this virtue? Tamil possesses gracious words with which even necessary criticism may be expressed without wounding the person addressed. What use was that? Sekkizhar apparently could not find them. Like choosing unripe fruit when ripe fruit is present, he abandoned pleasant language and abused the Jains with hurtful words.

Why? Did Sekkizhar have a personal feud with the Jains? No. The unalterable Saivite attachment lodged within him made him revile those eminent Jains with whatever words entered his mouth. For those who stand not upon the path of love but upon unnatural religious and scriptural paths, speaking or writing with sweetness and simplicity becomes impossible. Thus even people distinguished in intellectual inquiry, once caught in the net called religion, become intoxicated by religious fanaticism and are driven to do people harm rather than good.

What kind of people were these Jains subjected to Sekkizhar’s abuse? Who can deny that Tamil people today are able to live with some degree of dignity and knowledge because of the unforgettable great service rendered by eminent Jains? Who will deny that the Sangam works composed by Jain scholars stand as ships that rescue Tamils struggling in the mire of Aryan mythology and draw them toward the shore of knowledge? Who can deny that the Sangam literature now among us was produced by distinguished Jain poets? Yet these benefactors are the very people our champion of the righteous path, Lord Sekkizhar, calls deceivers, base men, outcastes, shameless men, sinners, evil-doers, senseless men, mean-headed men, workers of cruel deeds, and thugs.

Even supposing the Jains did us many harms, is it not a noble Tamil quality to forget those harms in view of the good they did?

> Even if one inflicts suffering equal to killing,  
> Recollection of the one good deed he did will efface it.

This does not mean Tamils should support those who do evil. It means that good done by them must not be forgotten. But did Sekkizhar, as a sign that he had not forgotten the Jains’ good, praise them anywhere, in even one place, in his *Periyapuranam*? Never.

Even if he did not praise them, did he at least follow the rule,

> Examine virtue and examine fault;  
> Weigh which is greater and accept what predominates,

and state in a single verse whether their merits or faults were greater? Search as much as one may, nothing can be found. Just as seawater tastes of salt wherever it is scooped up, wherever the *Periyapuranam* speaks of Jains it degrades, condemns, and abuses them. Not even inadvertently is there one word praising them for the good they did.

Instead of *Periyapuranam*, could such a book not have been named *The Purana of Abuse against the Jains*? Instead of *Thondar Puranam*, could it not at least have been called *The Purana of Jain Thugs*? Siva, embodiment of love, who granted authority to abuse the Jains shamelessly and ungratefully as “thugs,” would surely not have forbidden naming the work *The Purana of Abuse* or *The Purana of Thugs*.

Is it the act of the wise to coat life-destroying poison with sugar and confer sanctity upon it, or to conceal a work written with the aim of abusing particular people by naming it a devotional book and attempting to deceive humanity? I ask devotees who become exhausted and enraged at scholars demanding that such a stained book be cast into the fire to examine carefully the arguments I have made.

In the fourth part of my study of the *Periyapuranam*, I have explained what Sekkizhar said about Mangayarkkarasiyar, wife of the Pandya, whom Sambandar praised, and Kulachirai, the Pandya’s minister. Their histories may be seen there. Here I explain only one detail Sekkizhar gives about Mangayarkkarasiyar.

When he says that Mangayarkkarasiyar and Kulachirai saw auspicious omens as soon as Sambandar left for Madurai, Sekkizhar describes Mangayarkkarasiyar’s refinement. How?

> To the Pandya queen whose loins resemble  
> The hood of a growing serpent...

That is, Mangayarkkarasiyar possessed loins shaped like a snake’s spreading hood. Here Sekkizhar has become Kambar’s elder brother. How?

Although Kambar retold Valmiki’s epic as a devotional work and made its Ayodhya king Rama into a god, it was fundamentally an epic, not a devotional book. The *Periyapuranam*, however, is devotional at its foundation. Just as the *Kamba Ramayanam* took Valmiki’s *Ramayana* as its source, the *Periyapuranam* took the *Upamanyu Bhaktavilasa* as its source; yet both the *Upamanyu Bhaktavilasa* and the *Periyapuranam* were founded upon devotion. Valmiki’s *Ramayana* was not. Kambar conferred on Rama a divinity Valmiki had not given him and turned the epic into a devotional work fit for worship.

Perhaps that is why Kambar and Sekkizhar, intoxicated with devotion, competed in openly displaying women’s “most intimate secrets” so that the Lord’s devotees might behold them and become ecstatic. It seems to be the convention that once any book is made devotional, it must expose women’s private parts without concealment; otherwise it cannot qualify as devotional. Does not the *Kamba Ramayanam* demonstrate this? Why else do descriptions absent from Valmiki’s *Ramayana* abound in Kambar’s? Most devotional books seem to have been written to bring disgrace upon women.

Mangayarkkarasiyar in the *Periyapuranam* is a Tamil woman, and its poet was a Tamil man. Yet Sekkizhar arranged the story as though she were a blemished woman. Even if we do not count it a fault that he says she followed a path opposed to her husband’s, his statement that she took Kulachirai as her guardian is truly a cruel act degrading Tamil women.

That Kulachirai was her guardian is expressed in the summary verse of the *Periyapuranam*:

> A fence to the heavenly crop that was the Pandya queen.

As a fence is indispensable support for a crop, Kulachirai is thus said to have been an indispensable, fence-like support to Mangayarkkarasiyar. A place is fenced to prevent what is inside from being stolen or destroyed. What did Mangayarkkarasiyar possess that needed such protection? Only her chastity. Not only for her but for all women who regard chastity as their precious possession, it is something to be protected. Women must and can protect their own chastity; another person other than a husband cannot stand beside them and preserve it. To claim otherwise is a grave error. Yet here Kulachirai, not her husband but her husband’s minister, is said to have been Mangayarkkarasiyar’s guardian.

Perhaps one might suppose that because she was more beautiful than other women, her husband Nedumaran appointed his minister Kulachirai to guard her against abduction. Even that would violate Tamil custom. The practice of appointing a man as guardian to a woman belongs to Aryans, not Tamils. This is shown by the Aryan Rama appointing his brother Lakshmana to guard his wife Sita, whereas Ravana of Tamil tradition appointed women such as Trijata, Vibhishana’s daughter, to attend the Sita he is said to have abducted.

Even today, women accompany women and men accompany men. After marriage, a woman’s husband is regarded as her guardian and companion, while women serve as companions and guardians to an unmarried woman. Yet Sekkizhar, a Tamil, says that while Mangayarkkarasiyar’s husband was alive, Kulachirai, not even her kinsman, was her guardian. This is not merely an unforgivable offence; it insults Tamil tradition itself.

Some devotees of Rama may ask: “How was it wrong for Rama to appoint his brother Lakshmana as guardian to his wife Sita? When Sita was alone in the forest without female company and Rama went out, whom else could he appoint? Can this be called a fault, and can one ask why Ravana assigned women to Sita while Rama assigned a man?”

That is true, and we accept it. Ravana had the means to assign female attendants, while Rama did not, so he appointed his brother. To those who say that was not wrong, I wish to say one thing.

Kaikeyi caused Dasaratha to order Rama into the forest so her son Bharata might be crowned. Rama went. Learning this, Sita said she too would come, and he took her. Rama’s brother Lakshmana also went. Dasaratha did not order Sita and Lakshmana into exile; he commanded Rama alone. But Sita, unwilling to remain apart from her husband, and Lakshmana, unable to live separated from his elder brother, said, “We too shall come. Shall you live troubled in the forest while we live comfortably in the country? Never. We shall accompany you.” Rama agreed and took them.

But he forgot one thing. What? He could have said to Sita: “Woman, I am going to a forest where people do not move about. If you come alone, you may suffer; bring one or two women as companions. Living in the forest, I shall roam seeking fruit and roots for food. Can you accompany me everywhere? Even if you wish to share my suffering equally as a sign of lovers living united, there are natural occasions when women must go apart, and it would not be proper for me, though your husband, to stand with you then. Bring at least one woman to accompany you when you must withdraw for such needs, especially at night in the forest.”

Had Sita wished to take a woman companion, neither Dasaratha nor Kaikeyi would have prevented it. Had they intended to prevent anyone accompanying Rama, they would have allowed no one but him to depart. Even if they permitted Sita because she was his wife, they would have stopped Lakshmana. They did not.

Nor can it be claimed that, distressed by Dasaratha’s command, Rama’s mind was so unsettled that he failed to notice who accompanied him or whether any woman came with Sita. He appears to have possessed firm composure. When Sita said she would accompany him, Rama is said to have told her, “If you come to the forest, first give all your possessions in charity to Brahmins.” He was “clear-minded” enough not to say, “Give your property to anyone in need,” but to identify one community and command that all her possessions be given only to them. It cannot be said that he lacked the clarity and thought to ask his wife to bring a female companion. Devotees of Rama therefore cannot place this fault upon me. Let that rest.

It is impossible, for any reason, to suppose that Nedumaran, Mangayarkkarasiyar’s husband, appointed his minister to guard his wife. The *Periyapuranam* itself shows that this Pandya was a true Tamil; that for this reason he rejected Saivism, foreign to the Tamil path, and embraced the religion of the Jains who composed rare and great works producing knowledge, strength, and courage among people; and that his wife Mangayarkkarasiyar and minister Kulachirai opposed him, protected Saivism, and impaled the Jains. It is therefore plain that the king could never have made such an arrangement.

The *Periyapuranam* contains another undeniable indication that Mangayarkkarasiyar followed a path opposed to her husband and strayed from proper conduct. When Mangayarkkarasiyar and Kulachirai went to welcome Sambandar after hearing that he had arrived in Madurai, she is said to have been afraid to meet him and to have stood aside. Sekkizhar explains it with the phrase:

> Without going forward to meet him, she withdrew to one side.

Why did Mangayarkkarasiyar, instead of going forward and bowing to Sambandar whom she regarded as her guru, stand to one side in fear and shyness? Sambandar was young, a child according to the legend; Mangayarkkarasiyar was older, of an age to be his mother. Yet she was afraid to see the guru who was like a son to her and withdrew. Why? This incident indicates that her heart was not pure.

The *Periyapuranam* offers still another indication that her heart was not innocent and without deceit and that her conduct had departed from the proper path. When she set out to receive Sambandar, she did not say where she was going or for what purpose. What did that “good woman” say instead?

There are some women in our country who, when they wish to go somewhere on private business, do not tell their household the destination or what they intend to do there. There are many kinds of business: some may be spoken of and some may not. They customarily say they are going to an elder sister’s house, a younger sister’s house, or the pond. How could they state the truth?

People of differing faiths, however, do not ordinarily conceal their movements from one another. Valliyammal going to the Subramania temple does not lie to her husband Swamipillai, who goes to St. Joseph’s church, and hide where she is going. Their difference in doctrine need cause no quarrel, weariness, or discord. They need not even debate, “Only the god I worship is true and supreme; yours is false and inferior.”

But Mangayarkkarasiyar is represented as having to conceal her belief even from her husband. Why did this condition arise? If her attachment to Saivism were genuine, she need not have hidden it. She could have said, “My beloved husband, I believe in Saivism. The eminent Saivite teacher Tirugnanasambandar is now coming to Madurai, and I am going to see and worship him.” Nedumaran’s history shows that he was not a brute who delighted in wounding others’ feelings.

More plainly, if Mangayarkkarasiyar truly loved both her husband and Saivism, she should have discussed the matter with him before joining Kulachirai in arranging to invite Sambandar to Madurai for a religious contest with the Jains. A pure-hearted woman acts openly toward her husband and does not conceal her deeds; one may therefore understand that she has no falsehood, deception, or intrigue. But if she performs deeds without her husband’s knowledge, how can she be described as a woman of unfailing moral conduct?

Sekkizhar forgot that Mangayarkkarasiyar in this history was a Tamil woman. Though himself a Tamil son, he not only composed a purana to spread in Tamil Nadu a religion foreign to Tamils, but constructed stories in a manner degrading women. This is a grave and intolerable offence.

This essay, one portion of a study of the *Periyapuranam*, was therefore written to demonstrate that books composed in the name of devotion truly corrupt people’s conduct and especially degrade women; that Kambar and Sekkizhar accomplished this task skilfully; and that such books lead people away from the proper path.

15-9-1946

---NOTES---
The Tamil source is retained verbatim in the bilingual document. The title is intentionally sarcastic and is rendered accordingly. Quotations from the *Tirukkural* are translated for meaning; their traditional couplet numbers are not printed in the source. The phrase describing Mangayarkkarasiyar’s body and the essay’s extended claims about women, chastity, caste, religion, and ethnic tradition reflect the author’s polemical argument and period language; they are translated without endorsement. `மறைக்காடு` is identified as Vedaranyam, and `உபமன்யு பக்தவிலாசம்` is transliterated as *Upamanyu Bhaktavilasa*. OCR punctuation and a few damaged characters in proper names were resolved in English from context, while the Tamil OCR block remains unchanged.
